<h2 id="about">What this tool does</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><p>JSON is read by machines but edited by humans, and a single missing comma or stray quote can stop an entire config from loading. This formatter takes any JSON you paste and either beautifies it — one field per line, indented at 2 spaces, 4 spaces or a tab — or minifies it down to the tightest possible bytes for shipping to a browser or an API. On top of that it validates your input and, when the JSON is broken, tells you the exact line and column of the failure in plain words instead of a cryptic stack trace.</p><p>Developers wiring up an API response, students checking an assignment file and analysts tidying a config all need the same thing: a reliable, instant answer to 'is this valid, and if not, where?'. A key-sorting option rewrites every object so keys line up alphabetically, which makes two JSON files diffable side by side and turns a messy dump into something readable. Input and output sizes are shown as bytes so you can watch a minify step shrink a payload.</p><p>The whole job runs in your browser tab, so sensitive data — API keys, user records, a production config — is never uploaded to a validation service. Long inputs are handled in chunks with a progress bar so the page stays responsive on a multi-megabyte file, and the formatted result can be copied straight back with one click.</p></div>



<h2 id="how-to-use">How to use the JSON Formatter <span style="color:var(--cy)">| Step by Step Guide</span></h2>
<div class="howto"><ol><li><span>Paste your JSON into the box — raw API output, a config file or anything in between.</span></li><li><span>Choose 'Pretty print' or 'Minify' from the mode dropdown.</span></li><li><span>For pretty output pick your indent (2 spaces, 4 spaces or a tab) and toggle key sorting.</span></li><li><span>Press 'Format JSON'; if it is invalid, read the line and column shown in the error box.</span></li><li><span>Use 'Copy result' to take the formatted JSON, and watch the input vs output byte counts.</span></li></ol></div>
<div class="t3d-textbox ru" data-ru lang="ros"><p>Apna JSON box me paste karein, mode chunein (Pretty print ya Minify), chahein to indent (2/4/tab) aur keys sort ka option set karein. 'Format JSON' dabane par sahi JSON khoobsurati se format ho jata hai; agar ghalt ho to line aur column parhat clear error milta hai. Copy se result le lein — data browser se bahar nahi jata.</p></div>
<h2 id="features">Features</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><ul class="feat-list"><li>Pretty-print with a choice of 2-space, 4-space or tab indent</li><li>One-click minify to the smallest valid JSON</li><li>Human-readable validation error pinpointing line and column</li><li>Alphabetical key sorting so configs diff cleanly</li><li>Input vs output byte size shown with T.bytes formatting</li><li>Chunked, progress-tracked processing that keeps huge files responsive</li></ul></div>
<h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><ul><li>When the error gives a line and column, count back to that spot — a trailing comma before a } is the single most common JSON bug.</li><li>Sort keys before comparing two versions of a config file; identical content then produces an identical diff.</li><li>Minify the JSON you ship to users, but keep a pretty copy in version control — readable history beats one long line.</li><li>Paste a response body here instead of a logging service when it holds API keys or personal data.</li><li>Use minify mode to check how much a pretty config really costs in bytes before you embed it.</li></ul></div>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<details><summary>Does it fix invalid JSON for me?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>No, it finds the exact problem and tells you where it is (line and column) so you can correct it yourself. Guessing and auto-patching would silently change what your data means, which this tool avoids on purpose.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Why is my large JSON slow or frozen?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Parsing a many-megabyte file is genuinely heavy work. The page reads and scans the text in chunks with a progress bar so the tab stays responsive, but a very large single value still needs one big parse step.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Is my data uploaded for validation?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>No. The browser's own JSON.parse does the validation locally, so a config full of secrets or a user export never leaves the tab — there is no request to intercept.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>What does 'sort keys' change?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>It rewrites each object so its properties appear in alphabetical order (arrays keep their order). Values are untouched, and the sorted output is functionally identical JSON.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Is the json formatter free to use?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Yes — JSON Formatter has no usage cap, no sign-up and no watermark, and it is not a trial: the page is a static file on https://wafasmart.tech with no billing backend behind it. </p></div></details>
<details><summary>Should I process sensitive data here?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Because nothing leaves the tab, JSON Formatter is a reasonable choice for CNICs, payslips and bank statements. Two habits add safety: close the tab afterwards so page memory is released, and keep the original file — editing produces a new document rather than modifying the one you own.</p></div></details>
